window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "Prime-producing polynomials", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-1", "text": "1.1.1 Prime-producing polynomials § Try a few more values of \\(n\\). For \\(n=3,4,5\\), the expression gives \\(53,61,71\\), again all prime. In fact, it gives a prime for every whole number from zero through thirty-nine. Forty successful cases make the pattern look promising. Then the next value breaks it: \\[40^2+40+41=1681=41\\cdot41.\\] This number has a divisor other than one and itself, so it is not prime. There was no mistake in the earlier calculations. The mistake would have been to conclude from them that the expression must always produce a prime."}, {"title": "Counterexamples and universal claims", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-2", "text": "1.1.2 Counterexamples and universal claims § The claim “this expression produces a prime for every nonnegative integer” includes numbers we have not tried. Checking more values can give us more evidence, but there will always be another value beyond the ones we checked. A single failure has a different effect. The value \\(n=40\\) is enough to disprove the claim, because the claim said every value would work. We call such a failure a counterexample . Examples are still useful. They help us notice patterns and formulate conjectures—claims we suspect are true. But establishing a conjecture requires an argument that covers every case it makes a claim about. If there are only finitely many cases, checking them all may suffice. When there are infinitely many, we need a reason that applies without examining them one at a time."}, {"title": "Proof of closure under addition for even integers", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-3", "text": "1.1.3 Proof of closure under addition for even integers § Consider another familiar pattern: adding two even integers gives an even integer. We could try \\(2+4\\), \\(6+8\\), and many more sums, but we would face the same difficulty. How could those examples establish the claim for every pair? The definition of an even integer gives us a way forward. Any two even integers can be written as \\(2m\\) and \\(2n\\), where \\(m\\) and \\(n\\) are integers. Their sum is \\[2m+2n=2(m+n).\\] Since \\(m+n\\) is an integer, the sum is twice an integer and therefore even. We did not choose particular values for \\(m\\) and \\(n\\), so the argument applies to every pair of even integers. This is a proof. The proof works because each step has a justification. We used the meaning of “even,” the distributive law to factor out two, and the fact that a sum of integers is an integer. A reader can inspect these steps and see why the conclusion follows."}, {"title": "Axioms and inference rules", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-1", "text": "1.2.1 Axioms and inference rules § If we ask why the distributive law is valid, we can appeal to properties of arithmetic. But an argument cannot keep appealing to earlier arguments without ever having a starting point. At some stage, we must state the assumptions from which we are reasoning. These starting assumptions are called axioms . We must also say how conclusions may be drawn. For example, if we have established a statement \\(P\\), and also established that \\(P\\) implies \\(Q\\), we may conclude \\(Q\\). A rule of this kind is called a rule of inference . A formal theory specifies a language for writing statements, a collection of axioms, and rules of inference. A formal proof is a finite sequence of statements, each justified by an axiom or by a rule applied to earlier statements. In ordinary mathematical writing, we leave out many routine steps so that the main idea is easier to follow. Formalization makes those steps explicit. Its purpose is to make it possible to check whether an argument follows the rules, even when finding that argument required considerable insight."}, {"title": "Hilbert’s program", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-2", "text": "1.2.2 Hilbert’s program § In the early twentieth century, David Hilbert sought to put mathematics on secure foundations by making its axioms and proof rules explicit. An important part of his program was to establish that the resulting rules could not lead to contradictions. A theory is consistent if it cannot prove both a statement and its negation. Without this requirement, the rules could support conflicting conclusions. In ordinary classical logic, a contradiction would even allow every statement to be derived. A further ambition is that the axioms should settle every statement in their language: either the statement has a proof, or its negation does. A theory with this property is called complete . Consistency and completeness make different demands. A theory might never contradict itself and still leave some questions unanswered. There is also a question about methods. Given a statement, could we always determine whether it has a proof by following a fixed set of instructions? If such a procedure always stops with the correct answer, provability in the theory is decidable . This related decision problem brings computation into the discussion. It asks how to obtain an answer, rather than only whether the axioms supply one."}, {"title": "Proof checking, proof search, and decidability", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-3", "text": "1.2.3 Proof checking, proof search, and decidability § Suppose someone hands us a proposed proof. We can inspect each line and check its justification. If we find an invalid step, we have rejected that particular argument. We have not shown that the conclusion has no proof; another argument might succeed. There is a simple, if impractical, way to search for a proof. Assume proofs are written as finite strings over a finite alphabet and can be checked mechanically. List all candidate strings in order of length, using a fixed order for strings of the same length, and test them. If a proof exists, it will eventually appear. If no proof exists, however, the search has no reason to stop. After any finite number of failed attempts, longer candidates remain. This is why searching does not automatically give a decision procedure. It also does not prove that deciding is impossible: a different method might detect the absence of a proof without searching through candidates. Completeness would help. We could alternate between searching for a proof of the statement and searching for a proof of its negation. In a complete theory, one search must eventually succeed. In a consistent theory, they cannot both succeed. Together with mechanically checkable proofs, these properties would give us a way to settle each question."}, {"title": "Euclid’s algorithm", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-1", "text": "1.3.1 Euclid’s algorithm § To find the greatest common divisor of \\(48\\) and \\(18\\), we could list their divisors and compare the lists. Euclid’s algorithm avoids that work. Divide the larger number by the smaller one, then replace the pair by the smaller number and the remainder: \\[48=2\\cdot18+12,\\qquad18=1\\cdot12+6,\\qquad12=2\\cdot6+0.\\] The pairs are therefore \\[(48,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12)\\longrightarrow(12,6)\\longrightarrow(6,0).\\] When the second number is zero, the first is the answer: six. There are two things to justify. First, the replacement must preserve the common divisors. If \\(a=qb+r\\), any divisor of both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) also divides \\(r=a-qb\\). Conversely, a divisor of both \\(b\\) and \\(r\\) divides \\(a=qb+r\\). Thus the greatest common divisor stays the same. Second, the process must stop. Each nonzero remainder is smaller than the preceding positive divisor. A decreasing sequence of nonnegative integers cannot continue indefinitely. The procedure therefore reaches zero and returns the correct answer."}, {"title": "Configurations and transitions", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-2", "text": "1.3.2 Configurations and transitions § Imagine pausing the calculation after the first division. To resume it, we need the current pair, \\((18,12)\\), and the instruction to repeat the division step. The earlier pair, \\((48,18)\\), is no longer needed to continue. A configuration is a record of everything needed to resume a calculation. What belongs in it depends on how closely we describe the procedure. At the beginning of Euclid’s repeated step, the two numbers are enough because we already know which instruction comes next. If we pause halfway through a division, we may also need temporary values and a record of our place in the instructions. A transition takes one configuration to the next. A particular execution, or run , is a sequence of these transitions. The four pairs above describe one run of Euclid’s algorithm. The algorithm itself is the rule that governs every allowed input pair, together with the condition telling it when to stop."}, {"title": "Turing machines", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-3", "text": "1.3.3 Turing machines § Euclid’s algorithm uses instructions such as “divide and find the remainder.” To describe computation in general, it helps to break instructions into simpler operations. A Turing machine provides one way to do this. The machine has a tape divided into cells, a head that reads one cell at a time, and a finite set of internal states. A rule specifies what to do for each combination of the current state and the symbol being read: write a symbol, move the head one cell, and enter a new state. For example, a machine could scan a block of ones by repeatedly moving right while it reads a one, then halt when it reaches a blank. More elaborate rule tables can carry out arithmetic. A configuration records the tape contents, the head position, and the internal state, so it determines which rule applies next. The tape has no fixed limit on available space. This does not mean that a finite calculation uses infinite memory: after finitely many steps, the head has visited only finitely many cells. The model allows more space whenever it is needed, while keeping each individual step simple and precisely specified."}, {"title": "Program encodings and universal computation", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-4-1", "text": "1.4.1 Program encodings and universal computation § A Turing machine’s rule table is finite, so we can write it as a string of symbols. That string can be placed on the tape of another machine, along with an input. The second machine can read the rules and simulate the first machine, one step at a time. A universal machine does this for any correctly encoded machine and input. There is no need to build a different physical device for every calculation. Changing the encoded instructions changes the computation. The same idea underlies a stored-program computer. The Church–Turing thesis says that any calculation carried out by an effective mechanical procedure can be performed by a Turing machine. Different proposed accounts of computation, including systems based on function substitution, have turned out to describe the same computable functions. This agreement supports the thesis. It is not a proof covering an independently defined collection of every conceivable procedure. Universality concerns what can be computed, not how efficiently. In the standard model, quantum computers do not make uncomputable functions computable. Their importance lies in the possibility of changing the resources required for tasks that are already computable."}, {"title": "The halting problem", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-4-2", "text": "1.4.2 The halting problem § Some programs halt; others run forever. Euclid’s algorithm halts because its positive remainders keep decreasing. A program instructed simply to repeat the same action forever does not. Can one procedure determine which behavior occurs for any supplied program and input? Suppose such a procedure exists. Call it \\(H(P,x)\\), where \\(P\\) is the description of a program and \\(x\\) is its input. We require \\(H\\) itself to halt on every input and give the correct answer. Using \\(H\\), construct a new program \\(D\\). On input \\(p\\), it asks whether the program described by \\(p\\) halts when given \\(p\\) itself as input. If \\(H(p,p)\\) says yes, \\(D\\) enters an endless loop. If it says no, \\(D\\) halts. Now give \\(D\\) its own description, \\(d\\), as input. If \\(H(d,d)\\) predicts that this run halts, the instructions of \\(D\\) make it run forever. If \\(H(d,d)\\) predicts that it runs forever, the instructions make it halt. Either prediction is wrong. The contradiction comes from assuming that \\(H\\) always gives the right answer. No such general procedure exists. This does not prevent us from proving that a particular program halts, as we did for Euclid’s algorithm. It rules out one method that correctly settles every case and always finishes."}, {"title": "Incompleteness and undecidability", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-4-3", "text": "1.4.3 Incompleteness and undecidability § The halting argument gives a precise example of a question that no algorithm can settle in every case. It also helps clarify what it means for Hilbert’s ambitions to have limits. Gödel’s incompleteness results concern a fixed theory. Suppose its axioms can be generated by an algorithm, it is consistent, and it includes enough arithmetic to reason about integer calculations. Then some sentences can be neither proved nor refuted from those axioms. Adding a new axiom may settle a particular sentence, but if the enlarged theory still meets the conditions, it too has unsettled sentences. Under the usual additional conditions, such a theory also cannot prove its own consistency. A different question concerns logical validity. First-order logic lets us write statements about objects and their relations using phrases such as “for every” and “there exists.” A statement is valid if it holds under every permitted interpretation of those objects and relations. For example, in a nonempty collection, “if every object has a property, then some object has that property” is valid. Reversing the implication is not. Church and Turing showed that no algorithm always decides whether an arbitrary first-order statement is valid. Gödel’s completeness theorem for first-order logic does guarantee that every valid statement has a logical proof. There is no contradiction: finding proofs of valid statements does not tell a search when to stop on invalid ones. Nor does proving every logically valid statement mean that a chosen set of arithmetic axioms settles every statement about integers. These results limit general methods without undermining the proofs and algorithms that do work. For a task we can solve, further questions remain: how should we represent the input, how much work will the calculation take, and what information will its steps preserve? Those questions lead from computability to the design of computations."}, {"title": "Binary encodings", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-1", "text": "2.1.1 Binary encodings § A finite input can be represented by bits once an encoding is chosen. An integer may use binary digits; a string may encode characters; a graph may list edges. Encoding determines input length, which later affects resource counts. At a fixed length \\(n\\), there are \\(2^n\\) possible inputs. If outputs have a fixed bound, they too can be encoded, with padding if necessary. We first study a function on this finite domain. A procedure handling arbitrary lengths requires a systematic family of such functions, not a separate unrelated construction for every length."}, {"title": "Boolean functions and truth tables", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-2", "text": "2.1.2 Boolean functions and truth tables § A Boolean function \\(f:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\) specifies a yes/no answer for each bit string. Its truth table lists all inputs and their outputs. For OR on two bits, the outputs for \\(00,01,10,11\\) are \\(0,1,1,1\\). A multi-bit answer is a collection of Boolean output functions. This description specifies what to compute but not how. It hides neither an algorithm nor an efficiency guarantee: writing the full table already takes exponentially many rows in \\(n\\). We need a way to turn the specification into operations."}, {"title": "Polynomial representation over the binary field", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-3", "text": "2.1.3 Polynomial representation over the binary field § Work modulo two: addition is XOR, so \\(1+1=0\\), and multiplication is AND. For a row \\(s\\), define a detector \\[\\delta_s(x)=\\prod_{i:s_i=1}x_i\\prod_{i:s_i=0}(1+x_i).\\] It equals one exactly when \\(x=s\\). Therefore \\[f(x)=\\bigoplus_{s:f(s)=1}\\delta_s(x).\\] On any input, at most one included detector is one, so XOR gives the required answer. Expanding produces a polynomial in the input bits. For OR, the expansion simplifies to \\(x+y+xy\\). Substituting \\(x=y=1\\) gives \\(1\\), as required; ordinary integer addition would give the wrong interpretation."}, {"title": "Polynomial evaluation by circuits", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-1", "text": "2.2.1 Polynomial evaluation by circuits § A circuit evaluates this polynomial by connecting operations: multiply factors with AND, add terms with XOR, and use fixed bits for constants. Wires carry intermediate bits; a gate’s outputs become later inputs. The circuit has no directed cycles, so its gates can be evaluated in order. This construction proves that every fixed-size Boolean function has a circuit. It may use exponentially many gates. Universality—being able to express every function—is a different claim from being able to express every function economically."}, {"title": "NAND universality", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-2", "text": "2.2.2 NAND universality § NAND returns \\(\\neg(a\\land b)\\). It can build the operations needed above: tying its inputs gives \\(\\neg a\\), and negating a NAND output gives AND. XOR uses four NANDs: \\[t=\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b),\\quad u=\\operatorname{NAND}(a,t),\\quad v=\\operatorname{NAND}(b,t),\\quad a\\oplus b=\\operatorname{NAND}(u,v).\\] Checking the four input pairs verifies the last identity. Since NAND constructs AND, XOR, and negation, it evaluates the polynomial for any Boolean function. Classical fan-out is allowed here: the same bit may feed several gates. This is not yet a reversible construction. Four NAND gates compute XOR. Branch dots indicate shared classical inputs; small output circles indicate negation."}, {"title": "Irreversible Boolean operations", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-3", "text": "2.2.3 Irreversible Boolean operations § An AND output of zero could have come from \\(00\\), \\(01\\), or \\(10\\). If only the output remains, those inputs cannot be distinguished. The map is many-to-one and has no inverse. This is logical information loss, regardless of whether the gate was useful. A complicated circuit may implement a one-to-one overall input–output function while erasing internal records along the way. An overall reversible truth table does not by itself establish that each physical step is reversible or avoids dissipation."}, {"title": "Landauer’s principle", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-1", "text": "2.3.1 Landauer’s principle § A bit lives in a physical system. Resetting an unknown bit to zero merges two logical possibilities. Landauer’s principle assigns a minimum average heat cost \\(k_{\\mathrm B}T\\ln2\\) to erasing one unbiased bit in the usual thermal setting, without retaining a compensating record. Here \\(T\\) is temperature and \\(k_{\\mathrm B}\\) is Boltzmann’s constant. The conditions matter: known bits, biased distributions, and available correlations change the information that must be erased. The principle is not a fixed energy charge on every logical gate. Its role here is to connect lost information to physical resources. Further detail: Bennett: Landauer’s principle and reversible computation ."}, {"title": "Logical reversibility and dissipation", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-2", "text": "2.3.2 Logical reversibility and dissipation § A logically reversible map is one-to-one on its allowed states. It avoids the necessity of erasure at the level of that map, but a real implementation can still dissipate energy through resistance, friction, control, and imperfect operation. Conversely, an irreversible gate cannot be made logically invertible merely by operating it slowly. We must distinguish the mathematical map from its physical realization. Quantum unitary evolution will supply another reason for preserving information: it has an inverse. Measurement and interaction with an environment require a different description."}, {"title": "Reversible function embeddings", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-3", "text": "2.3.3 Reversible function embeddings § Any Boolean function can be embedded in a reversible operation by retaining its input: \\[U_f:(x,y)\\mapsto(x,y\\oplus f(x)).\\] Applying it twice restores \\(y\\), because XOR with the same bit twice cancels. With \\(y=0\\), the second register becomes the answer. Reversibility does not require \\(f\\) itself to be injective: the retained \\(x\\) distinguishes the original inputs. Extra bits are therefore not incidental bookkeeping. They are part of the state needed to make the full transformation one-to-one."}, {"title": "Controlled reversible operations", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-1", "text": "2.4.1 Controlled reversible operations § A controlled operation retains a control value while reversibly updating a target. CNOT maps \\((a,b)\\) to \\((a,b\\oplus a)\\). When \\(a=0\\), nothing changes; when \\(a=1\\), the target flips. In either case, the same operation undoes the change. Control is not synonymous with reversibility: the target operation must itself be reversible for every retained control value. This example introduces a useful design method—choose among invertible updates without discarding which update was chosen."}, {"title": "CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin gates", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-2", "text": "2.4.2 CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin gates § Toffoli maps \\((a,b,c)\\) to \\((a,b,c\\oplus ab)\\). A zero target records AND while preserving its inputs. Fredkin retains a control and swaps two targets when that control is one. Both are their own inverses. Toffoli with initialized workspace can implement general reversible Boolean computations; CNOT alone cannot produce the nonlinear term \\(ab\\). In circuit drawings a filled dot denotes a control on one, and a circled plus denotes an XOR target. A vertical connector means these symbols belong to one joint gate. CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin preserve their full inputs in reversible form. Each drawing shows one gate, with time running left to right."}, {"title": "Workspace and uncomputation", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-3", "text": "2.4.3 Workspace and uncomputation § A reversible calculation often yields \\((x,f(x),g(x))\\), where \\(g(x)\\) is temporary “garbage.” Resetting it would erase information. Instead, compute, XOR the result into a clean output register, and run the computation backward: \\[(x,0,0)\\to(x,f(x),g(x))\\to(x,f(x),g(x);f(x))\\to(x,0,0;f(x)).\\] The notation groups workspace schematically; only the copied answer remains in the fresh register. In a quantum computation this copies basis labels coherently, not an arbitrary unknown state. Uncomputation becomes essential when unwanted workspace records would obstruct interference. Compute the answer, copy its basis label to a fresh output, then apply the inverse computation. A may act on a larger workspace than the two schematic wires shown."}, {"title": "Outcomes, distributions, and frequencies", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-1", "text": "3.1.1 Outcomes, distributions, and frequencies § A classical bit has a value zero or one. If its preparation is uncertain, describe that uncertainty by probabilities \\(p_0,p_1\\geq0\\) with \\(p_0+p_1=1\\). Repeating the same preparation independently gives outcome frequencies that tend toward these probabilities; a short sample need not match them exactly. The distribution describes uncertainty across outcomes, not a fraction of a bit observed in one trial. This distinction between a predictive description and an individual result remains important in quantum mechanics."}, {"title": "Conditional probability and path weights", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-2", "text": "3.1.2 Conditional probability and path weights § Suppose a transition has conditional probabilities \\(P(j\\mid i)\\): the probability of next state j given current state i. The probability of a two-step path \\(i\\to j\\to k\\) is the product of its transition probabilities. To obtain the probability of reaching \\(k\\), sum over the disjoint intermediate alternatives: \\[P(k\\mid i)=\\sum_j P(k\\mid j)P(j\\mid i),\\] when the transition rule depends only on the current state. These are the product and sum rules for a Markov process. A fair randomizing step has every matrix entry \\(1/2\\); applying it twice still leaves a fair distribution."}, {"title": "Nondeterministic and probabilistic transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-3", "text": "3.1.3 Nondeterministic and probabilistic transitions § A nondeterministic model records permitted successors, not likelihoods. A computation accepts if some permitted branch accepts. Branch weights can be written as zero or one, but their combination uses logical AND along a path and OR across paths. Two successful paths still mean “possible,” not probability two. A probabilistic model instead weights branches and asks how likely acceptance is. Keeping these meanings distinct prevents a common confusion: quantum amplitudes are neither a list of permitted classical guesses nor ordinary probabilities."}, {"title": "A two-state quantum transformation", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-1", "text": "3.2.1 A two-state quantum transformation § Consider the Hadamard transformation \\[H=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] For a definite input zero, it produces amplitudes \\((1/\\sqrt2,1/\\sqrt2)^T\\); input one gives \\((1/\\sqrt2,-1/\\sqrt2)^T\\). Measuring the output in the zero–one basis gives each outcome with probability one half, the squared magnitude of either entry. For now take these as the predictions of a quantum operation. The negative sign is invisible in this single measurement. The next calculation shows why it cannot be discarded from the state description."}, {"title": "Composition of transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-2", "text": "3.2.2 Composition of transitions § Matrix multiplication gives \\(H^2=I\\). Starting at zero, two successive Hadamards return zero with certainty. The fair randomizing classical step does not: its square is still a fair randomizer. The comparison assumes no measurement between the quantum steps. Inserting a measurement after the first Hadamard destroys the coherence needed for the certain return. We are not claiming that all classical devices lack hidden internal information; rather, a model that retains only the two outcome probabilities cannot reproduce this coherent composition. No measurement occurs between the two Hadamards. Their product is the identity."}, {"title": "Signed amplitudes and outcome probabilities", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-3", "text": "3.2.3 Signed amplitudes and outcome probabilities § Amplitudes combine before they are converted to probabilities. Two paths contributing \\(+1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\) to the same final outcome sum to zero, hence give zero probability. Squaring each first would instead give \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\), a different prediction. The order of operations matters: \\[p(k)=\\left|\\sum_{\\text{paths to }k}\\text{path amplitude}\\right|^2.\\] This rule applies to alternatives that remain coherent. If a measurement or environment keeps a distinguishable record of the alternatives, we must include that record in the joint description rather than summing as if it were absent."}, {"title": "Sum and product rules", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-1", "text": "3.3.1 Sum and product rules § For two Hadamards with input zero, the path through intermediate zero contributes \\(1/2\\) to final zero, and the path through intermediate one contributes another \\(1/2\\). The final amplitude is one. For final one, the contributions are \\(1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\), summing to zero. Thus both outcomes are accounted for, and the probabilities sum to one. This is matrix multiplication read as a path calculation: \\((H^2)_{k0}=\\sum_j H_{kj}H_{j0}\\). The intermediate label is a bookkeeping basis, not necessarily an observed trajectory. Multiply along each route, then add contributions to the same final label. Paths through the two intermediate labels are coherent alternatives."}, {"title": "Constructive and destructive interference", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-2", "text": "3.3.2 Constructive and destructive interference § Constructive interference means contributions reinforce; destructive interference means they partly or completely cancel. Neither creates probability from nothing. A unitary transformation redistributes amplitudes while preserving their total squared magnitude. An algorithm must arrange reinforcement at useful outcomes and cancellation elsewhere. Merely creating many nonzero amplitudes does not accomplish this. The two-Hadamard example is small enough that every term can be inspected, but the same sum-of-products structure governs a register with many basis labels."}, {"title": "Path information and interference", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-3", "text": "3.3.3 Path information and interference § Write a state vector as a ket, \\(\\ket r\\); the overlap of two vectors is written \\(\\braket{r_0}{r_1}\\). If the two intermediate alternatives leave orthogonal records \\(\\ket{r_0}\\) and \\(\\ket{r_1}\\), the final amplitude contributions are attached to different joint states. Their squared norms add rather than cancel. More generally, the interference term is proportional to the overlap \\(\\braket{r_0}{r_1}\\). Identical records preserve full interference; orthogonal records remove it from the unobserved-register statistics. Chapter 5 develops these joint states. For now the practical lesson is that information left elsewhere matters even when nobody reads it. Observation by a conscious observer is not required."}, {"title": "Relative and global phase", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-1", "text": "3.4.1 Relative and global phase § A sign is a special case of phase. Write an amplitude as \\(re^{i\\phi}\\), where \\(r\\geq0\\) is its magnitude. Its outcome probability is \\(r^2\\). Multiplying every amplitude by the same phase changes no prediction: it is a global phase. Changing one amplitude’s phase relative to another can alter interference. For example, \\((\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) gives equal zero–one probabilities for every \\(\\phi\\), but a subsequent Hadamard gives zero with probability \\((1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\)."}, {"title": "Complex numbers and phase representation", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-2", "text": "3.4.2 Complex numbers and phase representation § The complex number \\(i\\) satisfies \\(i^2=-1\\). For \\(z=a+ib\\), its conjugate is \\(z^*=a-ib\\), and \\(|z|^2=z^*z=a^2+b^2\\). Complex addition combines directed contributions in a plane; multiplication multiplies magnitudes and adds phases. This makes complex numbers convenient for coherent path weights. Conjugation matters because probabilities must be real and nonnegative: squaring \\(i/\\sqrt2\\) gives \\(-1/2\\), whereas its squared magnitude is \\(1/2\\)."}, {"title": "State vectors and transition matrices", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-3", "text": "3.4.3 State vectors and transition matrices § Choose an ordering of outcomes and collect amplitudes in a column \\(\\psi\\). Put the amplitude for \\(i\\to j\\) in matrix entry \\(U_{ji}\\): column \\(i\\) is the result of the transformation on basis input \\(i\\). Then \\(\\psi'=U\\psi\\), and successive steps give \\(VU\\psi\\), with the rightmost operation acting first. The matrix is a compact description of the same path rules. Quantum states require normalization and physical transformations require additional constraints; the next chapter states those requirements and relates coordinates to measurements."}, {"title": "Basis coordinates and ket notation", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-1", "text": "4.1.1 Basis coordinates and ket notation § In a chosen orthonormal basis, a qubit has coordinates \\(\\psi=(\\alpha,\\beta)^T\\). Dirac notation writes the vector as \\(\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\), where \\(\\ket0=(1,0)^T\\) and \\(\\ket1=(0,1)^T\\). The ket brackets name a vector, not a new operation. A basis label names a possible outcome of the corresponding basis measurement. A superposition is a linear combination relative to that basis; the same vector can itself be a basis vector in another basis. “Being in a superposition” therefore requires specifying the reference basis."}, {"title": "Complex inner products and bra notation", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-2", "text": "4.1.2 Complex inner products and bra notation § For complex vectors, define \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi=\\sum_j\\phi_j^*\\psi_j\\). The bra \\(\\bra\\phi\\) is the conjugate-transpose row that performs this operation. Conjugation ensures \\(\\braket\\psi\\psi=\\sum_j|\\psi_j|^2\\geq0\\). For a unit vector \\(\\phi\\), the overlap is the coefficient of the component along \\(\\phi\\); the projected vector is \\(\\ket\\phi\\braket\\phi\\psi\\). The coefficient can be complex, so a picture of a real-plane shadow illustrates only a restricted case. Orthogonality means zero overlap and will correspond to perfectly distinguishable basis alternatives."}, {"title": "Changes of basis", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-3", "text": "4.1.3 Changes of basis § Let \\(\\ket\\pm=(\\ket0\\pm\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). These form an orthonormal basis. The state \\(\\ket0\\) is also \\((\\ket++\\ket-)/\\sqrt2\\). Its coordinates changed, not its physical preparation. If the columns of \\(B\\) are the new basis vectors expressed in old coordinates, the new coordinates are \\(B^\\dagger\\psi\\). This passive change of description must be distinguished from physically applying a gate. Applying \\(B^\\dagger\\) and then measuring in the old basis does, however, implement a measurement in the new basis. That connection will become useful in circuits."}, {"title": "Pure states and normalization", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-1", "text": "4.2.1 Pure states and normalization § The pure-state postulate represents a system by a unit vector in a complex inner-product space, with vectors differing only by global phase representing the same physical state. Thus \\(\\sum_j|\\alpha_j|^2=1\\). A qubit can be written \\(\\cos(\\theta/2)\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\sin(\\theta/2)\\ket1\\). The squared magnitudes specify one measurement’s probabilities, while relative phase affects other measurements. Not every preparation has a pure-state description: classical uncertainty about preparation or entanglement with another system can require a density matrix. The main text first treats pure preparations and complete joint systems."}, {"title": "Projective measurement and conditional states", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-2", "text": "4.2.2 Projective measurement and conditional states § For a projective measurement with orthogonal projectors \\(P_j\\) summing to identity, the Born rule gives \\[p(j)=\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2.\\] Conditional on an outcome with nonzero probability, the state becomes \\(P_j\\ket\\psi/\\sqrt{p(j)}\\). For one-dimensional projectors \\(P_j=\\ket{e_j}\\bra{e_j}\\), this is measurement along orthonormal directions. If \\(\\ket\\psi=(\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\), the outcomes have probabilities \\(3/4\\) and \\(1/4\\). One run produces one outcome, not both fractional answers. Restoring unit length describes the state after conditioning on that outcome. A real-coordinate illustration of the Born rule. The projections are components used to calculate probabilities, not simultaneous fractional measurement results."}, {"title": "Repeated measurements on independently prepared states", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-3", "text": "4.2.3 Repeated measurements on independently prepared states § Repeated measurements on independently prepared copies estimate a distribution. If an event has probability \\(p\\), its count in \\(N\\) independent trials has mean (expected count) \\(Np\\) and variance (mean squared deviation from that count) \\(Np(1-p)\\). The observed fraction has typical fluctuation of order \\(1/\\sqrt N\\), not exact equality to \\(p\\). This differs from measuring the same system twice without re-preparing it: after an ideal projective measurement, the same outcome is certain on immediate repetition in the same measurement. An experiment must specify both preparation and measurement before its counts can be interpreted."}, {"title": "Operator action on a basis", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-1", "text": "4.3.1 Operator action on a basis § A linear transformation is determined by its action on basis vectors: \\(A\\sum_j\\alpha_j\\ket j=\\sum_j\\alpha_j A\\ket j\\). Those basis images are its matrix columns. For example, a real-plane shear with columns \\((1,0)^T\\) and \\((1,1)^T\\) moves the second basis direction while retaining the first. It illustrates matrix construction but is not a valid deterministic isolated-system quantum evolution because it does not preserve all lengths and overlaps. Linear algebra describes many maps; the physical postulate selects which ones describe closed evolution."}, {"title": "Adjoints and inner products", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-2", "text": "4.3.2 Adjoints and inner products § After applying \\(A\\) to a state, its overlap with reference \\(\\phi\\) is \\(\\braket\\phi{A\\psi}\\). The adjoint is defined by \\[\\braket\\phi{A\\psi}=\\braket{A^\\dagger\\phi}\\psi.\\] We can transform the state and keep the reference fixed, or transform the reference by the adjoint and keep the state fixed. In coordinates \\(A^\\dagger=(A^*)^T\\). For a rotation, the adjoint rotates backward. For a real stretch \\(A=\\operatorname{diag}(2,1)\\), it is the same stretch, not its inverse. The overlap identity, not “undoing,” is the general meaning of the adjoint."}, {"title": "Unitary evolution", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-3", "text": "4.3.3 Unitary evolution § The evolution postulate assigns a unitary \\(U\\) to a closed system’s evolution: \\(U^\\dagger U=I\\). Consequently, \\[\\braket{U\\phi}{U\\psi}=\\braket\\phi\\psi.\\] Norms, total probability, and distinguishability through overlaps are preserved; the inverse is \\(U^\\dagger\\). For finite-dimensional linear maps on a fixed state space, preservation of every vector’s norm implies this condition. Normalizing each output separately after an arbitrary map would generally be nonlinear and does not establish a physical evolution rule. Measurement is not such an invertible map on the measured system; its outcomes and any environment must be treated separately."}, {"title": "Eigenvectors and eigenspaces", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-1", "text": "4.4.1 Eigenvectors and eigenspaces § An eigenvector satisfies \\(A\\ket v=\\lambda\\ket v\\). Its direction is preserved; its coefficient changes by \\(\\lambda\\). For \\(Z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)\\), the basis vectors have eigenvalues \\(+1,-1\\). For \\(X\\), which exchanges zero and one, \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) have those eigenvalues. A vector in a unitary eigendirection gains only a phase, since the eigenvalue has magnitude one. Eigenvectors therefore identify especially simple responses to an operation and help us choose useful input states."}, {"title": "Hermitian operators and spectral projections", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-2", "text": "4.4.2 Hermitian operators and spectral projections § A Hermitian operator \\(A=A^\\dagger\\) has real eigenvalues and an orthonormal eigenbasis. Grouping equal eigenvalues gives the spectral decomposition \\(A=\\sum_j a_jP_j\\). In the associated ideal measurement, the numerical result is \\(a_j\\) and the probability is \\(\\|P_j\\psi\\|^2\\). Degeneracy means an outcome selects a subspace, not a unique vector. This makes precise the phrase “measure an observable”: specify both the eigenspaces used for projection and the numerical labels reported. Changing an observable can change outcome probabilities without changing the input preparation."}, {"title": "Nonorthogonal state discrimination", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-3", "text": "4.4.3 Nonorthogonal state discrimination § Perfectly distinguishing two pure states requires an outcome that identifies each with certainty. Such a measurement separates them into orthogonal outcome subspaces, so their overlap must be zero. Equivalently, a unitary interaction that produces perfectly distinct records cannot turn a nonzero initial overlap into zero, because unitaries preserve overlaps. Hence \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\), whose overlap is \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), cannot always be identified correctly from one unknown copy. This limit is structural, not a defect of a particular detector. General measurements can trade errors for inconclusive outcomes, but cannot evade this deterministic limit."}, {"title": "Tensor products and product bases", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-1", "text": "5.1.1 Tensor products and product bases § Two qubits have joint basis labels \\(00,01,10,11\\). Write \\(\\ket{ab}=\\ket a\\otimes\\ket b\\), using the tensor product. Coordinates are ordered here with the first qubit as the leftmost bit. For individual vectors \\((a,b)^T\\) and \\((c,d)^T\\), their tensor product is \\((ac,ad,bc,bd)^T\\). Every pair of local coordinates contributes one joint coordinate. Dimensions multiply rather than add. An operation on the first qubit alone is \\(U\\otimes I\\); it acts on each pair of coefficients with the second label held fixed."}, {"title": "Product states", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-2", "text": "5.1.2 Product states § Independent pure preparations give a product state \\(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket\\phi\\). The amplitude of a joint basis outcome is the product of the two local amplitudes, so its probability is the product of their probabilities. For example, \\(\\ket+\\otimes\\ket0=(\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\). Although two joint amplitudes are nonzero, the second qubit is certainly zero and the state factors. Superposition alone is not evidence of entanglement. The relevant question is whether one local vector for each subsystem reproduces every joint amplitude."}, {"title": "The composition postulate", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-3", "text": "5.1.3 The composition postulate § The composition postulate assigns the tensor-product space to a composite system. A register of \\(n\\) qubits therefore has \\(2^n\\) basis coordinates. This is a statement about the mathematical description, not a promise that a measurement yields \\(2^n\\) numbers. A basis measurement returns one \\(n\\)-bit label. Maintaining a general state vector on a classical simulator can be expensive, but that fact alone does not prove an algorithmic advantage. We must still identify a task and a process that makes the required information observable efficiently."}, {"title": "Bell-state preparation", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-1", "text": "5.2.1 Bell-state preparation § Prepare \\(\\ket{00}\\), apply \\(H\\) to the first qubit, then CNOT with the first as control. The state changes as \\[\\ket{00}\\to\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}}{\\sqrt2}\\to\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{11}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] The final vector is a Bell state, denoted \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). The circuit drawing records this sequence, with time running left to right. The vertical CNOT connector acts on the joint state; it does not mean that the first qubit was measured to decide a classical action on the second. A Hadamard followed by CNOT prepares a Bell state. Read the circuit from left to right. Experiment: build a Bell state Follow the joint state from \\(\\ket{00}\\) through a Hadamard on the first qubit and then CNOT. The four bars show computational-basis probabilities. Apply H to the first qubit Reset State: |00⟩ Both qubits start in 0. The final pure state is entangled: its joint amplitudes do not factor into two single-qubit states. Its probabilities alone would not distinguish it from a classical mixture."}, {"title": "Nonfactorization of amplitudes", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-2", "text": "5.2.2 Nonfactorization of amplitudes § If \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) factored as \\((a\\ket0+b\\ket1)\\otimes(c\\ket0+d\\ket1)\\), its coefficients would require \\(ac=bd=1/\\sqrt2\\) but \\(ad=bc=0\\). The first two equations make all four factors nonzero, contradicting the latter equations. The state is entangled. This elementary test establishes more than correlation in one chosen measurement: it rules out every product of pure local states. A classical mixture of \\(00\\) and \\(11\\) has the same zero–one counts, but different predictions in other bases."}, {"title": "Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-3", "text": "5.2.3 Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities § For joint amplitudes \\(\\alpha_{ab}\\), \\(p(a,b)=|\\alpha_{ab}|^2\\). The marginal probability is \\(p(a)=\\sum_b p(a,b)\\); when \\(p(a)>0\\), \\(p(b\\mid a)=p(a,b)/p(a)\\). The Bell state gives \\(p(00)=p(11)=1/2\\) and equal local probabilities. It also equals \\((\\ket{++}+\\ket{--})/\\sqrt2\\), so equal X-basis outcomes are certain. The classical mixture of \\(00\\) and \\(11\\) instead gives independent fair X outcomes. Correlation alone in one basis does not establish coherent entanglement; the measurement choice matters."}, {"title": "Basis-state copying", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-1", "text": "5.3.1 Basis-state copying § CNOT maps \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) to \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\ket0\\) to \\(\\ket1\\ket1\\). Thus it copies a computational basis label into a blank target. On a superposition, linearity gives \\(\\alpha\\ket{00}+\\beta\\ket{11}\\), not \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)^{\\otimes2}\\). The latter generally contains cross terms \\(\\ket{01}\\) and \\(\\ket{10}\\). Keeping the label-copying calculation explicit prevents a misleading inference from two successful basis tests to copying arbitrary quantum information."}, {"title": "The no-cloning theorem", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-2", "text": "5.3.2 The no-cloning theorem § Suppose one unitary copied every unknown pure state into a blank register. For two possible inputs, overlap preservation would require \\[\\braket\\phi\\psi=(\\braket\\phi\\psi)^2.\\] For distinct nonorthogonal states the overlap is neither zero nor one, so the equation fails. The contradiction excludes a universal cloner. A device designed for a known orthogonal set can copy its labels; preparing a known state repeatedly also poses no problem. The restriction is on perfectly copying an arbitrary unknown state by one fixed operation."}, {"title": "Local states and no-signalling", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-3", "text": "5.3.3 Local states and no-signalling § A subsystem of an entangled pair need not have a pure-state vector. Its local predictions can be summarized by a density matrix; for either half of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), it is \\(I/2\\). A local unitary or a measurement with its outcome ignored cannot change the other party’s marginal distribution. Conditioning on a communicated outcome can change the recipient’s description, but the information must arrive through a classical channel. This is why entanglement supplies correlations without an instantaneous signalling method. Appendix D gives the density-matrix calculation."}, {"title": "Entanglement resources", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-1", "text": "5.4.1 Entanglement resources § Alice holds an unknown qubit \\(\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) and one half of a Bell pair; Bob holds the other half. The initial joint state is \\(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). Alice applies CNOT from the input to her Bell half, then H to the input. No quantum system carrying the input is then sent to Bob. The shared entanglement and two later classical bits are the resources. To see why the protocol works, we must track the complete state rather than assume the Bell pair already contains the answer."}, {"title": "Measurement and conditional corrections", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-2", "text": "5.4.2 Measurement and conditional corrections § After Alice’s gates, the state is \\[\\frac12\\sum_{m,n\\in\\{0,1\\}}\\ket{mn}\\otimes X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi.\\] Alice’s measurement gives each pair \\((m,n)\\) with probability one quarter. Bob’s conditional state is \\(X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi\\); applying \\(Z^mX^n\\) recovers \\(\\ket\\psi\\). In temporal order he applies \\(X^n\\) first, then \\(Z^m\\). The circuit uses dashed classical controls after measurement. The formula accounts for every outcome, so teleportation succeeds without selecting a favorable measurement result. Alice’s two measured bits control Bob’s X and Z corrections. Dashed lines carry classical information; the Bell pair is prepared at the left."}, {"title": "State transfer and classical communication", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-3", "text": "5.4.3 State transfer and classical communication § Before the two classical bits arrive, averaging Bob’s four possible conditional states gives \\(I/2\\), independent of \\(\\psi\\). He cannot extract a message about the input from that state alone. After correction, his qubit has the input state; Alice no longer retains that unknown state, and the original Bell pair has been consumed. Thus teleportation transfers quantum information without cloning it. It also illustrates a recurring discipline: distinguish a joint pure state, a state conditioned on a known result, and a local state when that result is unavailable."}, {"title": "Constant and balanced Boolean functions", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-1", "text": "6.1.1 Constant and balanced Boolean functions § Let \\(f:\\{0,1\\}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\). The task is to determine whether \\(f(0)=f(1)\\). Such a function is constant if they agree and balanced if they differ. For this one-bit domain these exhaust all possibilities; for larger domains, restricting attention to constant or balanced functions is a genuine promise. We need one property, not a printed list of both function values. This distinction between the requested answer and the full data guides the quantum algorithm."}, {"title": "Classical query access", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-2", "text": "6.1.2 Classical query access § A classical query chooses \\(x\\) and receives \\(f(x)\\). One query cannot settle this task with certainty: either observed value is compatible with both a constant and a balanced function, depending on the unqueried value. Two queries suffice. The comparison is exact and uses a specified access interface. It does not claim that every classical computation related to the function needs two physical operations. If the function’s description is supplied in a different form, the access model and the comparison may change."}, {"title": "Reversible quantum oracles", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-3", "text": "6.1.3 Reversible quantum oracles § The quantum interface is the reversible oracle \\[U_f\\ket{x,y}=\\ket{x,y\\oplus f(x)}.\\] On basis states it permutes labels and is its own inverse, so its linear extension is unitary. The input register is retained. A query means one use of this specified operation, even when the input is a superposition. The black-box model counts uses without charging for the internal construction of \\(U_f\\). This abstraction isolates information access; later circuit analysis must restore costs hidden inside the box."}, {"title": "Oracle action on superpositions", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-1", "text": "6.2.1 Oracle action on superpositions § With a clean target, a query gives \\[U_f\\left(\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\right)=\\frac{\\ket{0,f(0)}+\\ket{1,f(1)}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] This follows directly from linearity and the basis definition. There is no step that individually reads both outputs into a classical memory. The state contains coherent relationships among amplitudes. Whether those relationships help depends on the next transformation and on which measurement is made."}, {"title": "Register measurement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-2", "text": "6.2.2 Register measurement § Measuring both registers immediately returns one random pair \\((x,f(x))\\). It does not return the two values required for the classical exact solution. Preparing the same state again can yield more samples, but sampling is not the desired one-query advantage. A useful algorithm must convert the property “equal or different” into a distinction between measurable final states. Simply evaluating in superposition has not finished the computation."}, {"title": "Workspace entanglement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-3", "text": "6.2.3 Workspace entanglement § If \\(f(0)\\ne f(1)\\), the target stores orthogonal records of the two inputs. Applying H only to the input cannot make those distinct joint alternatives cancel each other as though their target records were identical. Their contributions must still be grouped by the complete output label. This is the same path-record effect introduced in Chapter 3. Rather than discard the record, we can choose a target preparation that returns to itself while transferring the function information into phase."}, {"title": "Bit-flip eigenstates", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-1", "text": "6.3.1 Bit-flip eigenstates § The state \\(\\ket-=(\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) satisfies \\(X\\ket-=-\\ket-\\). Therefore a conditional bit flip multiplies this target by a sign without changing its physical state. If no flip occurs, the sign is positive. This eigenvector property is precisely what the oracle’s target needs. It is not enough to call the target a “minus state”; the calculation \\(X(\\ket0-\\ket1)=\\ket1-\\ket0\\) supplies the reason it transfers a bit value into a sign."}, {"title": "Phase-oracle construction", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-2", "text": "6.3.2 Phase-oracle construction § For each input label, \\[U_f\\ket x\\ket-=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\ket-.\\] With input \\(\\ket+\\), the result is \\([(-1)^{f(0)}\\ket0+(-1)^{f(1)}\\ket1]\\ket-/\\sqrt2\\). The target factors out in the same state for every input, so it carries no distinguishable record of which branch occurred. The signs now belong to the input amplitudes. Equal signs differ from two positive signs only by a global phase; opposite signs are a relative phase difference. This is phase kickback. The target returns to the same minus state, while the input receives a function-dependent phase."}, {"title": "Coherent uncomputation", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-3", "text": "6.3.3 Coherent uncomputation § For a general predicate computed using workspace, apply a phase to its computed answer and then reverse the computation. If \\(A\\ket{x,0}=\\ket{x,f(x),g(x)}\\), the combined operation \\(A^\\dagger Z_{\\mathrm{answer}}A\\) maps \\(\\ket{x,0}\\) to \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\ket{x,0}\\). Workspace is restored while the sign remains. This is a phase-oracle construction from a value computation, not a free replacement: it uses the computation and its inverse. Counting only the final phase box would conceal those implementation costs."}, {"title": "Interference and readout", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-1", "text": "6.4.1 Interference and readout § Apply H to the input after kickback. Writing \\(s_x=(-1)^{f(x)}\\), the resulting input state is \\[\\frac{s_0+s_1}{2}\\ket0+\\frac{s_0-s_1}{2}\\ket1.\\] Equal signs give outcome zero with certainty; opposite signs give outcome one with certainty. The final Hadamard combines the two alternatives so that the unused outcome cancels. This equation shows both the output and the mechanism: the relative phase, rather than either function value separately, determines the answer."}, {"title": "Quantum and classical query counts", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-2", "text": "6.4.2 Quantum and classical query counts § Deutsch’s algorithm prepares \\(\\ket0\\ket1\\), applies H to each qubit, queries \\(U_f\\), applies H to the input, and measures that input. It distinguishes constant from balanced using one quantum query, whereas an exact classical method needs two. Both statements concern the same oracle task and demand certainty in the ideal model. The circuit’s other gates and preparation are not queries, but they are real operations. A query separation is a clean first example of quantum advantage, not a complete hardware performance claim. Deutsch’s algorithm: prepare, query, recombine, and measure. Only the joint U_f box counts as an oracle query. Experiment: an oracle changes relative phase Prepare the input in \\(\\ket{+}\\) and the target in \\(\\ket{-}\\). The oracle leaves the target unchanged and multiplies each input amplitude by \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\). A final Hadamard makes the relative sign observable. Function f(0) = 0, f(1) = 0 f(0) = 1, f(1) = 1 f(0) = 0, f(1) = 1 f(0) = 1, f(1) = 0 Apply oracle Reset |0⟩ +1/√2 |1⟩ +1/√2 Both input amplitudes have the same sign. Choose a function, then apply its oracle. A common sign changes no probabilities. Opposite signs change the result after the final Hadamard."}, {"title": "Preparation, oracle evaluation, and measurement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-3", "text": "6.4.3 Preparation, oracle evaluation, and measurement § The reusable strategy is to prepare coherent alternatives, encode a relevant property in relative amplitudes, recombine them, and measure in a basis that exposes the distinction. Correctness requires tracing the state through all four stages, including workspace. A circuit can be short and still fail if the final states for different answers are not distinguishable. The next chapter uses a related idea for a different task: finding a marked input. A phase mark will again be the beginning, not the completed answer."}, {"title": "Search predicates and classical queries", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-1", "text": "7.1.1 Search predicates and classical queries § Search a set of \\(N\\) labels using a predicate \\(f(x)\\) that is one on marked items. An ordinary query tests a candidate. With one uniformly located marked item, classical search needs order \\(N\\) queries for constant success probability; checking all items settles the worst case. Begin with \\(N=4\\) and mark \\(11\\). We count access to the predicate, while keeping state preparation and other operations visible. The task is to output a marked label, not merely estimate the number of them."}, {"title": "State preparation and phase marking", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-2", "text": "7.1.2 State preparation and phase marking § Two Hadamards prepare \\(\\ket s=(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{11})/2\\). A phase oracle \\(O_f\\ket x=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\) changes the amplitudes to \\((1,1,1,-1)/2\\). Every squared magnitude is still one quarter. Measuring now gains nothing. The oracle has created a relative sign that a subsequent operation can use. A phase oracle may be supplied directly or built by kickback or compute–phase–uncompute; its query cost must be stated accordingly."}, {"title": "Reflection and final amplitudes", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-3", "text": "7.1.3 Reflection and final amplitudes § Apply \\(D=2\\ket s\\bra s-I\\). For an amplitude list \\(a_x\\), it sends each entry to \\(2\\mu-a_x\\), where \\(\\mu\\) denotes the arithmetic mean of the entries. After marking, the mean is \\(1/4\\): the three \\(+1/2\\) entries become zero, and the \\(-1/2\\) entry becomes one. One iteration \\(DO_f\\) therefore finds the marked label with certainty. The operation is a reflection about the prepared direction; it is not a measurement that estimates the mean from samples. A complete two-qubit search for 11. The first controlled-Z marks 11. The remaining coherent gates implement diffusion up to global phase. Both measured bits are one."}, {"title": "Good and bad subspaces", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-1", "text": "7.2.1 Good and bad subspaces § Suppose \\(0"}, {"title": "Initial success amplitude", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-2", "text": "7.2.2 Initial success amplitude § Write \\(\\sin\\theta=\\sqrt{M/N}\\), with \\(0<\\theta<\\pi/2\\). Then \\(\\ket s=\\sin\\theta\\ket G+\\cos\\theta\\ket B\\). Its initial success probability is \\(\\sin^2\\theta=M/N\\). The geometry uses the bad direction as angle zero and the good direction as angle \\(\\pi/2\\). This choice fixes signs in the rotation calculation. Although the underlying register may have many amplitudes, the two reflections preserve this plane, so one angle is enough to track the entire search evolution."}, {"title": "Reflections and rotations", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-3", "text": "7.2.3 Reflections and rotations § The oracle negates the good component and retains the bad component: a reflection across the bad axis. The diffusion operation reflects across \\(\\ket s\\). Acting in this order, their product increases the state’s angle from the bad axis by \\(2\\theta\\). Reflection matrices also verify the result algebraically. A geometric drawing is useful because it explains both the increase in success probability and its eventual decrease: a rotation continues past the good axis unless we stop it."}, {"title": "Amplitude evolution", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-1", "text": "7.3.1 Amplitude evolution § After \\(k\\) Grover iterations, \\[\\ket{\\psi_k}=\\sin((2k+1)\\theta)\\ket G+\\cos((2k+1)\\theta)\\ket B,\\qquad p_k=\\sin^2((2k+1)\\theta).\\] The initial angle contributes one \\(\\theta\\); each iteration contributes two more. All marked labels have equal amplitudes in this uniform-search setting, so conditional on success the result is uniform over the marked set. The success probability depends on the marked fraction, not on which labels happen to be marked."}, {"title": "Iteration count and overshooting", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-2", "text": "7.3.2 Iteration count and overshooting § Choose a nonnegative integer \\(k\\) near \\(\\pi/(4\\theta)-1/2\\) to bring the first peak close to the good axis. For a small marked fraction, \\(\\theta\\approx\\sqrt{M/N}\\), yielding order \\(\\sqrt{N/M}\\) iterations. Integer rounding usually prevents exact certainty; the four-item, one-mark case is special. More iterations are not always better. With \\(N=4,M=1\\), the probabilities for \\(k=0,1,2\\) are \\(1/4,1,1/4\\). This illustrates why the stopping rule is part of the algorithm rather than an implementation detail. Experiment: rotate toward the marked item There is one marked item. A full Grover iteration rotates the state by \\(2\\theta\\), where \\(\\sin\\theta=1/\\sqrt N\\). Keep stepping after the first peak to see why overshooting matters. Search space 4 16 64 256 One iteration Play Reset Iterations 0 unmarked component → marked component ↑ Probability of finding the marked item: 6.25%. The initial state spreads probability uniformly over all items."}, {"title": "Unknown marked fraction and verification", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-3", "text": "7.3.3 Unknown marked fraction and verification § If the marked fraction is unknown, a fixed peak estimate may overshoot. A standard approach chooses a random iteration count from a gradually expanding range, measures, and verifies the candidate. For \\(M>0\\), suitable schedules retain expected order \\(\\sqrt{N/M}\\) query cost. If \\(M=0\\), verification never succeeds; an unbounded search cannot certify absence. A bounded-error decision version uses a bounded schedule and reports failure with a specified error guarantee when marked items exist. Distinguish that guarantee from an exact proof that none exists. If all items are marked, direct sampling already succeeds. Further detail: Boyer, Brassard, Høyer, and Tapp: Tight bounds on quantum searching ."}, {"title": "Arbitrary state preparation", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-1", "text": "7.4.1 Arbitrary state preparation § Uniform preparation is not essential. Let \\(A\\ket0=\\ket\\psi\\), and let \\(P_G\\) project onto the states counted as successful. If \\(0"}, {"title": "Reflection using preparation and its inverse", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-2", "text": "7.4.2 Reflection using preparation and its inverse § Reflection about the prepared state is \\[R_\\psi=2\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi-I=A(2\\ket0\\bra0-I)A^\\dagger.\\] Applied to an existing state, the rightmost inverse acts first. Pair this with \\(I-2P_G\\), which negates successful components. Their product rotates toward the good direction just as in search. The construction requires coherent access to \\(A\\), its inverse, and the success test. A destructive measurement of success is not a replacement for the coherent phase reflection. The inverse preparation acts first. Conjugating the zero-state reflection produces reflection about the prepared state."}, {"title": "Success probability and resource cost", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-3", "text": "7.4.3 Success probability and resource cost § For small \\(p\\), direct independent trials need order \\(1/p\\) preparations for constant success probability. Amplitude amplification needs order \\(1/\\sqrt p\\) iterations, each using the preparation, its inverse, and a coherent success test. If their costs are \\(C_A\\) and \\(C_G\\), the resulting cost is of order \\((C_A+C_G)/\\sqrt p\\), under the stated access assumptions. Verification and iteration selection must also be included. The quadratic improvement is in repeated use of these resources; expensive state loading or oracle construction can dominate the total runtime."}, {"title": "Wires and gate ordering", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-1", "text": "8.1.1 Wires and gate ordering § A circuit displays a sequence of operations with time increasing left to right. Each horizontal wire identifies a subsystem; a box denotes an operation. A wire does not carry a definite classical value when it belongs to a superposition or an entangled register. To interpret a circuit, track the joint vector between columns. If the first gate is \\(U\\) and the next is \\(V\\), the final state is \\(VU\\ket\\psi\\). Gates on disjoint wires act by tensor products and commute with one another, though a particular hardware schedule may impose additional constraints."}, {"title": "Circuits for query and search algorithms", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-2", "text": "8.1.2 Circuits for query and search algorithms § The earlier algorithms now supply concrete reading exercises. In Deutsch’s circuit, the first H pair prepares the query state; the oracle encodes signs; the last H exposes their relation. In search, the repeated block consists of a phase oracle followed by reflection about the prepared state. Circuit notation records how to compose operations, but a box labelled \\(O_f\\) is still an abstract oracle until its implementation is specified. Drawing a box does not prove its cost is one elementary gate."}, {"title": "Measurement and classical control", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-3", "text": "8.1.3 Measurement and classical control § A measurement symbol terminates coherent evolution of that measured output within the diagram’s description and produces a classical result. Dashed lines here carry classical bits controlling later operations; solid lines carry quantum systems. A classical conditional correction means that the experimenter chooses a gate using a known outcome. It differs from a coherent controlled gate acting on an unmeasured superposition. Teleportation uses both: a coherent CNOT before measurement and classically controlled corrections afterward."}, {"title": "Pauli, Hadamard, and rotation gates", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-1", "text": "8.2.1 Pauli, Hadamard, and rotation gates § The basic matrices are \\[X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad Z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad H=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Also \\(Y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}\\). For any Pauli matrix \\(P\\), define \\(R_P(\\theta)=\\cos(\\theta/2)I-i\\sin(\\theta/2)P\\). Since \\(P^2=I\\), this is unitary. The phase gates \\(S=\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)\\) and \\(T=\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\pi/4})\\) provide useful fixed relative phases. These formulas define the gates without requiring calculus or matrix exponentials. Experiment: a gate changes amplitudes Start in \\(\\ket{0}\\). Apply \\(H\\) twice, then compare \\(HZH\\) with a single \\(X\\). Orange marks a negative amplitude; the bar length shows its squared magnitude. Apply H Apply X Apply Z Reset to |0⟩ State: 1 |0⟩ + 0 |1⟩ Choose a gate to change the state. This experiment uses real amplitudes. It illustrates X, Z, and H, not every possible qubit state."}, {"title": "Controlled and entangling gates", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-2", "text": "8.2.2 Controlled and entangling gates § A controlled-U is \\(\\ket0\\bra0\\otimes I+\\ket1\\bra1\\otimes U\\). It retains the control and conditionally transforms the target. CNOT is controlled-X; controlled-Z changes only the sign of \\(\\ket{11}\\). Conjugating the target of CNOT by H gives controlled-Z because \\(HXH=Z\\). Not every controlled operation creates entanglement on every input, but CNOT can do so on \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\). Arbitrary local gates alone preserve product structure; an entangling operation is needed for general joint transformations. Hadamards on the target convert controlled-X to controlled-Z."}, {"title": "Oracle and reflection circuits", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-3", "text": "8.2.3 Oracle and reflection circuits § A phase mark for a particular bit string can be built by flipping its zero positions, applying a phase flip controlled on all ones, and undoing those flips. A computed predicate can instead use compute–phase–uncompute. For uniform search, \\(D=H^{\\otimes n}(2\\ket{0^n}\\bra{0^n}-I)H^{\\otimes n}\\). The two-qubit drawing uses X gates and controlled-Z to realize the zero-state phase flip, producing \\(-D\\), which differs only by a harmless global phase in an uncontrolled Grover iteration. A controlled use would require tracking that phase explicitly. The oracle and reflection are decomposed here for one explicit predicate; a general predicate needs its own circuit."}, {"title": "Limits of local operations", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-1", "text": "8.3.1 Limits of local operations § A sequence made entirely of one-qubit gates has the form \\(U_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes U_n\\). On a product input it produces another product state. It therefore cannot prepare a Bell state from \\(\\ket{00}\\), regardless of how many local gates are used. This argument establishes a structural limitation without counting gates. Universal circuit construction needs a way for information in different subsystems to affect the joint state coherently."}, {"title": "Entangling gates and universality", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-2", "text": "8.3.2 Entangling gates and universality § Arbitrary single-qubit unitaries together with CNOT form a universal gate set for finite-qubit unitaries. One route to the theorem decomposes a matrix into two-level unitaries, each acting on a pair of basis vectors, and then implements those using controlled operations and local rotations. We state the synthesis theorem rather than prove every decomposition here. Its scope is expressibility, not efficiency: a generic operation on \\(n\\) qubits requires exponentially many parameters and generally many gates. Structured algorithmic operations are valuable because their descriptions permit economical constructions. Further detail: Barenco et al.: Elementary gates for quantum computation ."}, {"title": "Exact synthesis and approximation", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-3", "text": "8.3.3 Exact synthesis and approximation § A finite set such as Clifford+T generates a dense collection of unitaries, enabling approximation to any desired accuracy; it cannot express every continuously parameterized unitary exactly with a finite word. A useful target is operator-norm error \\(\\|U-V\\|\\leq\\epsilon\\), which bounds the output-vector error for every unit input. If a circuit contains \\(L\\) approximated gates, errors can be budgeted so their sum is at most the desired tolerance. Precision belongs in the resource analysis: a gate count without its accuracy requirement is incomplete."}, {"title": "Gate count, depth, and workspace", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-1", "text": "8.4.1 Gate count, depth, and workspace § Gate count counts elementary operations, depth counts sequential layers that cannot be run in parallel, and width counts qubits, including ancillas. These resources can trade off: extra workspace may reduce depth, while repeated recomputation may save space. Clean ancillas must be initialized and usually restored for reuse. A uniform circuit family has a classical procedure generating the circuit for each input length; efficiency requires that construction to be efficient too. Otherwise a short circuit might conceal difficult work in its specification."}, {"title": "Connectivity and data preparation", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-2", "text": "8.4.2 Connectivity and data preparation § An abstract circuit may connect any pair of qubits, while a device permits only nearby interactions. Routing can add SWAP gates, count, and depth. Preparing an arbitrary list of amplitudes may itself require work proportional to the list size; it cannot be assumed free because the final register is small. Likewise, the arithmetic that implements a predicate may outweigh the number of oracle calls. A resource estimate should specify gate set, connectivity, input preparation, and output extraction before translating an asymptotic advantage into an implementation claim."}, {"title": "Error accumulation and reliability", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-3", "text": "8.4.3 Error accumulation and reliability § Small coherent gate errors and stochastic noise can accumulate across a long circuit. Approximation error in ideal synthesis is different from physical noise, although both affect predictions. Reliable computation uses encoded logical information, syndrome extraction, and correction or fault-tolerant control. The no-cloning theorem does not forbid error correction: quantum codes encode one logical state into correlations, not independent readable copies. This book does not develop a noise model or threshold theorem. It identifies reliability as an additional resource requirement beyond proving an ideal algorithm correct."}, {"title": "Input size and success criteria", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-1", "text": "9.1.1 Input size and success criteria § A comparison begins with the same task, encoding, input size, permitted access, and success criterion. For an integer \\(N\\), binary input length is of order \\(\\log N\\), so a procedure taking \\(N\\) steps is exponential in that length. Distinguish worst-case, average-case, and expected costs; an expectation needs a stated distribution or algorithmic randomness. Query models also specify what one request returns. Changing the interface can change the problem, not merely the method used to solve it."}, {"title": "Asymptotic notation", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-2", "text": "9.1.2 Asymptotic notation § For eventually nonnegative functions, \\(T(n)=O(g(n))\\) means an eventual upper bound \\(T(n)\\leq Cg(n)\\) for some constant \\(C>0\\). The notation \\(\\Omega(g(n))\\) gives a corresponding lower bound; \\(\\Theta(g(n))\\) gives both. For example, \\(3n^2+7n+2=\\Theta(n^2)\\). These notations suppress constants, not the input encoding or the model. Polynomial bounds describe powers of input length; exponential bounds such as \\(2^n\\) grow much faster. A polynomial exponent can still make a method impractical at relevant sizes."}, {"title": "Query, gate, and space complexity", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-3", "text": "9.1.3 Query, gate, and space complexity § Query complexity counts oracle uses, gate complexity counts elementary operations, and space complexity counts memory. A quantum query advantage may survive or disappear when translated into total gate and preparation cost. Physical runtime also depends on scheduling, error correction, and device constraints. State which level a bound concerns. For unstructured search, the quadratic query reduction is a precise theorem. It is not a theorem that searching any stored dataset on any quantum device is quadratically faster end to end."}, {"title": "P, NP, and reductions", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-1", "text": "9.2.1 P, NP, and reductions § P consists of decision problems solvable deterministically in polynomial time. NP consists of those whose yes instances have polynomial-size witnesses verifiable in polynomial time. Equivalently, a nondeterministic machine can guess a witness and verify it; this is not a claim that a physical random guess will probably find one. A polynomial-time reduction from A to B transforms instances so that a solver for B yields a solver for A with polynomial overhead. It compares difficulty without claiming equal runtimes. Whether P equals NP remains unresolved."}, {"title": "BPP and BQP", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-2", "text": "9.2.2 BPP and BQP § BPP uses classical randomness and polynomial time, with error bounded by a constant below one half, conventionally one third. BQP uses uniformly generated polynomial-size quantum circuits with a comparable error bound. Repetition and suitable decision rules can reduce error when independent trials have a fixed gap above chance. Quantum circuits can implement classical randomized computation, giving \\(\\mathrm{BPP}\\subseteq\\mathrm{BQP}\\). It is not known that BQP contains all of NP, or that every BQP problem lies outside efficient classical computation. These are classes of decision tasks; factoring is usually posed as a function task."}, {"title": "Factoring and search", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-3", "text": "9.2.3 Factoring and search § Quantum period-finding methods give polynomial-time factoring algorithms in the binary input length, although their derivation lies beyond this book. No polynomial-time classical factoring algorithm is known; this is not a classical lower bound proving exponential advantage. Grover search, in contrast, has a proved quadratic separation in a specified unstructured query setting. These examples illustrate two kinds of evidence: an algorithm compared with currently known competitors, and an optimality statement within a restricted model. Neither implies that quantum computers accelerate every task."}, {"title": "Upper and lower bounds", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-1", "text": "9.3.1 Upper and lower bounds § An upper bound is established by an algorithm and a cost analysis. A lower bound must constrain every algorithm allowed by the model, including ones nobody has invented. Thus a slow known method does not establish a lower bound. Restricting to query access makes some general arguments possible: algorithms may perform arbitrary input-independent processing, but the hidden input influences them only through counted queries. A bound in this model can be strong and useful while saying nothing by itself about the cost of implementing a query."}, {"title": "Unstructured search optimality", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-2", "text": "9.3.2 Unstructured search optimality § For finding a unique marked item among \\(N\\) candidates with constant success probability, quantum algorithms require \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) queries. Grover’s \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\) algorithm matches this, so the query complexity is \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\). Intuitively, a query can change the state substantially only through amplitude placed on the marked location; averaged over possible locations, that amplitude is limited. A hybrid argument makes this accumulation precise. We state the lower-bound theorem here; the geometric derivation of Grover alone proves its upper bound, not its optimality."}, {"title": "Acceptance polynomials and approximate degree (optional)", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-3", "text": "9.3.3 Acceptance polynomials and approximate degree (optional) § An alternative method represents acceptance probability as a polynomial in Boolean oracle bits. After \\(T\\) standard queries, amplitudes have degree at most \\(T\\), and probabilities degree at most \\(2T\\). A bounded-error algorithm must therefore produce a low-degree polynomial approximating the desired Boolean function on every Boolean input. If the function’s approximate degree is \\(d\\), then \\(T\\geq d/2\\). The approximate degree of OR on \\(N\\) bits is \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\), yielding a matching lower bound for detecting a marked item. Appendix E explains the representation; the approximation theorem is an additional mathematical result. Further detail: Beals et al.: Quantum Lower Bounds by Polynomials ."}, {"title": "Algorithmic mechanisms and assumptions", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-1", "text": "9.4.1 Algorithmic mechanisms and assumptions § The algorithms in this book use coherent transformations to make a requested property affect outcome probabilities. Deutsch’s algorithm turns equality of function values into relative phase and then a definite bit. Amplitude amplification rotates a prepared state toward a successful subspace. Their advantages require specified oracles, controllable inverse operations where used, and a suitable final measurement. Large state spaces provide room for these constructions, but size alone is not their explanation. Correctness is established by the evolution and readout calculation."}, {"title": "Computability and readout limitations", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-2", "text": "9.4.2 Computability and readout limitations § Quantum computation does not remove the halting obstruction, permit arbitrary cloning, or expose all amplitudes through one measurement. It also does not eliminate the cost of encoding inputs, implementing operations, or protecting states from noise. These constraints are part of the model, not qualifications appended after the algorithm. A useful claim names the task, mechanism, success guarantee, and resources. The same discipline that distinguished a pattern from a proof at the start now distinguishes a plausible speedup from an established one."}, {"title": "Period finding and error correction: further topics", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-3", "text": "9.4.3 Period finding and error correction: further topics § Period finding develops a different use of interference: a Fourier transform converts periodic structure into measurable frequency information. Quantum error correction addresses the reliability of long computations through encoded subspaces and measured syndromes. Both build on the state, operator, measurement, and circuit language developed here, but require additional constructions and proofs. They are natural continuations rather than prerequisites to the present algorithms. The current foundation is complete enough to identify what those later chapters must explain: a mechanism, a correct readout, and an honest resource bound."}, {"title": "Automata and Memory", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-a", "text": "A. Automata and Memory A finite automaton stores one of finitely many control states while reading an input. Two states suffice for parity: reading one swaps “even” and “odd,” and reading zero leaves the state unchanged. Exact matching of arbitrarily many opening and closing symbols needs more memory. For the language \\(\\{a^nb^n:n\\geq0\\}\\), two different long prefixes of a’s must reach the same state in any fixed finite automaton. Appending the number of b’s matching one prefix then forces the same decision on one valid and one invalid string. This state-collision argument proves the limitation. A stack distinguishes arbitrarily many pending symbols. Push once for each a, then pop once for each b; accept when input and stack finish together in the correct order. A pushdown automaton formalizes this memory. A linear-bounded automaton has writable tape bounded by a constant multiple of input length. A Turing machine removes that fixed bound. More memory changes the configurations available, not the meaning of an individual step. These models explain different language classes; they are not successive hardware generations."}, {"title": "Grammars and Lambda Calculus", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-b", "text": "B. Grammars and Lambda Calculus A grammar specifies a start symbol, terminal symbols, nonterminal symbols, and replacement rules. The rules \\(S\\to aSb\\) and \\(S\\to\\epsilon\\) generate exactly matching a’s and b’s. Applying the first rule twice and then the second gives \\(aabb\\). Generation asks what can be produced; recognition asks whether a supplied string belongs to the generated set. Regular grammars correspond to finite automata, context-free grammars to pushdown automata, context-sensitive grammars to linearly bounded machines, and unrestricted grammars to Turing-recognizable languages, with the usual conventions about the empty string. Lambda calculus describes computation by function application and substitution. The reduction \\((\\lambda x.t)u\\to t[x:=u]\\) replaces free occurrences of x in t by u, renaming bound variables when necessary to avoid capture. For example, \\((\\lambda x.x)u\\to u\\). A Church numeral n is a function that applies a supplied function n times to a supplied starting value. Thus three is \\(\\lambda f.\\lambda x.f(f(f(x)))\\). Functions alone can encode data and iteration. Agreement with Turing computability is a mathematical equivalence between formal models; it supports, rather than defines away, the broader Church–Turing thesis."}, {"title": "Linear Algebra Reference", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-c", "text": "C. Linear Algebra Reference For complex coordinates, use \\(\\braket uv=u^\\dagger v\\), where \\(u^\\dagger=(u^*)^T\\). The norm is \\(\\sqrt{u^\\dagger u}\\). An orthonormal basis matrix B satisfies \\(B^\\dagger B=I\\), and converts old coordinates to new by \\(B^\\dagger\\). Matrix products act right to left on column vectors. The adjoint reverses their order: \\((AB)^\\dagger=B^\\dagger A^\\dagger\\). A unitary preserves overlaps; a Hermitian operator equals its adjoint; an orthogonal projector satisfies \\(P^2=P=P^\\dagger\\). These are different properties. Tensor products obey \\((A\\otimes B)(u\\otimes v)=Au\\otimes Bv\\) and \\(\\braket{u\\otimes v}{x\\otimes y}=\\braket ux\\braket vy\\). In the binary ordering used in this book, the leftmost qubit is the most significant label. Operator norm is \\(\\|A\\|=\\max_{\\|v\\|=1}\\|Av\\|\\). It is useful for uniform error bounds because \\(\\|(U-V)v\\|\\leq\\|U-V\\|\\) for every unit vector. These identities provide reference material; their operational meanings are introduced where used in the chapters."}, {"title": "Mixed States and General Measurements", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-d", "text": "D. Mixed States and General Measurements A density matrix records a preparation that need not be pure. An ensemble preparing \\(\\ket{\\psi_r}\\) with probability \\(q_r\\) has \\(\\rho=\\sum_r q_r\\ket{\\psi_r}\\bra{\\psi_r}\\). It is positive semidefinite and has trace one. A pure state has \\(\\rho=\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi\\); different ensembles can yield the same density matrix and therefore the same measurement predictions. The Born rule is \\(p(j)=\\operatorname{tr}(P_j\\rho)\\), and unitary evolution gives \\(U\\rho U^\\dagger\\). For a joint system, the reduced state is \\(\\rho_A=\\operatorname{tr}_B\\rho_{AB}\\): sum the B diagonal blocks in a product basis. Tracing one half of the Bell state gives \\(I/2\\). A trace-preserving local operation on B, with operators \\(M_r\\) satisfying \\(\\sum_rM_r^\\dagger M_r=I\\), leaves A’s reduced state unchanged when outcomes are ignored. This follows by inserting the sum into the partial trace. Conditioning on r changes the description only for someone who knows r. A general measurement has positive effects \\(E_j\\) summing to identity and probabilities \\(\\operatorname{tr}(E_j\\rho)\\). Effects specify probabilities but not the complete state update; measurement operators provide that additional information. For example, to distinguish either \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket+\\), choose \\(E_0=c\\ket-\\bra-\\), \\(E_+=c\\ket1\\bra1\\), and \\(E_?=I-E_0-E_+\\), with \\(c=1/(1+1/\\sqrt2)\\). The first outcome is impossible for \\(\\ket+\\), the second for \\(\\ket0\\), and the remaining outcome is inconclusive. Positivity of \\(E_?\\) follows because the largest eigenvalue of \\(\\ket-\\bra-+\\ket1\\bra1\\) is \\(1+1/\\sqrt2\\). Certainty when conclusive is purchased by sometimes declining an answer."}, {"title": "Polynomial Lower Bounds", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-e", "text": "E. Polynomial Lower Bounds In the Boolean query model, the hidden input is a string \\(z\\in\\{0,1\\}^N\\). A query transforms \\(\\ket{i,b}\\) to \\(\\ket{i,b\\oplus z_i}\\). Each output amplitude is a combination of previous amplitudes multiplied by \\(z_i\\) or \\(1-z_i\\). Starting from input-independent amplitudes, induction shows that T queries produce amplitudes of polynomial degree at most T in the input bits. Input-independent unitaries form linear combinations and do not increase this degree. An acceptance probability sums squared magnitudes of accepting amplitudes, so its degree is at most 2T. Restricting to Boolean inputs allows powers \\(z_i^k\\) with positive k to be replaced by \\(z_i\\). The approximate degree of a Boolean function is the least degree of a real polynomial approximating it to a chosen constant error below one half on every Boolean input. Hence query complexity is at least half that degree. Proving OR has approximate degree of order \\(\\sqrt N\\) requires an approximation-theoretic argument beyond this short derivation. The polynomial representation and that theorem together yield the search-decision lower bound; neither follows from observing that known algorithms are slow."}];
